Even Descartes' most severe critics — Hume, Kant, Nietzsche — attacked his interpretation of 'I', not the core logical move: thinking implies existence.
A hypothetical 'evil demon' deceiving you about everything would still need something to deceive — so even total sensory deception confirms the existence of a deceived mind.
The argument works equally for any conscious being: a different rational agent running through the same reasoning must arrive at the same conclusion for themselves.
Modern neuroscience confirms consciousness is computationally real — the experience of thinking corresponds to measurable neural activity, supporting the premise that thinking is an undeniable occurrence.
The Cogito is the starting point of Descartes' entire philosophical system — without it, nothing else he built would hold; its resilience has been tested across 380 years of philosophical scrutiny.
